A blast from the past! Delphi 7 is an old version of the Delphi programming language, but I'll do my best to help you with rotating a PDF file using Delphi 7.

To rotate a PDF file using Delphi 7, you'll need to use a third-party library that can handle PDF files. One popular option is the `iText` library, which is a free, open-source library for working with PDF files.

Here's an example code snippet that demonstrates how to use `iText` to rotate a PDF file using Delphi 7:
```delphi
program RotatePDF;

uses
  iText, iText.Forms, iText.Text;

var
  pdfFile, rotatedPdfFile: string;
  pdf: TPDFDocument;
  page: TPDFPage;
  angle: Integer;

begin
  pdfFile := 'input.pdf'; // input PDF file
  rotatedPdfFile := 'output.pdf'; // output PDF file

  // Create a new PDF document
  pdf := TPDFDocument.Create;

  // Open the input PDF file
  pdf.Open(pdfFile);

  // Set the page rotation angle
  angle := 90; // rotate 90 degrees clockwise

  // Iterate through each page in the PDF document
  for i := 0 to pdf.GetNumPages - 1 do
  begin
    page := pdf.GetPage(i);

    // Rotate the page
    page.Rotate(angle, 0, 0);

    // Write the rotated page to the output PDF file
    pdf.WritePage(page, rotatedPdfFile);
  end;

  // Close the output PDF file
  pdf.Close;

  // Release memory
  pdf.Free;
end.
```
This code creates a new `TPDFDocument` object, opens the input PDF file using the `Open` method, sets the page rotation angle using the `Rotate` method, and then writes the rotated pages to the output PDF file using the `WritePage` method.

Note that this code uses the `iText` library, which is not included with Delphi 7. You'll need to download and install the `iText` library separately. Also, this code assumes that you have the `iText.Forms`, `iText.Text`, and `iText.Forms.PDF` units included in your project

